  "slug": "effect-zero-field-wigner-e833",
  "summary": "Recent preprints examine crystallization and competing phases in 2D electron systems across bilayers, quantum wells, and graphene.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the competition between crystalline, liquid, and density-wave ground states in two-dimensional electron systems. In the mass-asymmetric electron-hole bilayer study, a microscopic Hartree-Fock-Bogoliubov analysis reports a roton minimum, with first-order crystallization preempting roton softening. Experiments on cadmium arsenide quantum wells probe the competition between zero-field Wigner solids and quantum Hall liquids and report low-temperature agreement. A renormalization-group study of rhombohedral n-layer graphene near van Hove singularities identifies emergent attraction and competing CDW and PDW orders. The three items differ in method and platform, so the shared element is the sensitivity of the ground state to competing orders rather than a single mechanism. What is new is the explicit treatment of zero-field crystallization alongside density-wave and pairing channels in these systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
